Evals — How I decided the output was good enough to ship

I tested the prototype against the sample positions and checked three things for each case:

Was the risk detected?
Was it prioritized correctly relative to other risks?
Was the explanation easy to understand without looking at the underlying calculation?
Test cases
#	What I tested	Expected result	Pass criteria
1	A position where margin used is more than 90% of available margin	Show a margin risk	The position appears in the risk list and the reason clearly explains the margin issue
2	A position expiring within 1–2 days	Show an expiry risk	The position appears and the reason mentions that expiry is close
3	A position making up more than 40% of portfolio value	Show a concentration risk	The position appears and the reason explains that too much exposure is concentrated there
4	A position with no risk trigger (e.g., TCS)	Do not show it as a risk	The position does not appear in the risk list
5	More than one risk present at once	Show the most important risks first	Higher-severity risks are placed higher in the list
6	Fewer than 3 risks found	Show only the actual risks	The app does not add unnecessary positions just to fill three cards
Definition of "good enough to ship" (for this prototype)
Every risk shown could be traced back to one of the three defined rules — no unexplained flags.
The app produced a short list, not a full positions dashboard.
Each risk's reason was understandable on its own, without needing to inspect the underlying math.
The suggested action gave the trader a clear next step to consider — without making the trading decision for them.

Explicitly not tested (outside scope for a prototype): live market data, broker integration, performance at scale.

What I'd test next

If I had more time, the next round of evals would look at:

Tie-breaking: what happens when two risks land at the same priority level?
Combined risk: what happens when a single position has both margin risk and expiry risk at once?
Drill-down: whether traders want to tap a risk to see more detail about why it was flagged.
Trust: whether traders actually find the suggested actions useful and trustworthy, or just noise.

These are the natural next hypotheses to validate before expanding scope beyond the current three risk types.
